# Plain language + first-use glosses (user-facing replies)

When writing a **reply to the user** (chat—not every comment inside code):

<h2 id="abbr-outline" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">Document outline</h2>

1. [Plain language default](#plain-default)
2. [Abbreviations and opaque labels](#abbr-expand)
3. [Technical terms and dense sentences](#tech-gloss)
4. [Exceptions](#exceptions)
5. [Examples](#examples)

---

<h2 id="plain-default" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">1. Plain language default</h2>

- Prefer **everyday wording** for judgments and tradeoffs (“giving the customer someone else’s fix by mistake” before “false reuse above threshold”).
- Use **technical terms only when they carry precision** the plain phrase would lose; then **gloss them** (section 3).
- Keep glosses **short**: a phrase or one short sentence inside parentheses—not a paragraph.

---

<h2 id="abbr-expand" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">2. Abbreviations and opaque labels</h2>

On **first appearance in that reply**, expand:

- **Decision IDs** (`D1`–`D7`), **version tags** (`V1`, `V1.1`, `V2`), **phase tags** (`P4`), **project acronyms** (`RRF`, `LGPD`), **skill/rule keywords** (`implref`, `aciubtdd`) when used as nouns.

**Format:** term immediately followed by parentheses with **1–2 sentences** (plain language + pointer if helpful).

- `D6 (promotion rule in the Samsung spec: only switch the default to hybrid search after A/B tests justify it)`

Do **not** re-expand the same abbreviation later in the same reply unless the reply is very long and clarity clearly needs a reminder.

Skip glossing **everyday dev terms** (`API`, `SQL`, `JSON`) unless this project gave them a special meaning.

---

<h2 id="tech-gloss" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">3. Technical terms and dense sentences</h2>

When you use a **technical term** the user may not carry from earlier turns, add a **layman gloss in parentheses** right after the term:

- `recall (how often the right past ticket shows up in the top results)`
- `false reuse (the agent copies a fix from the wrong old ticket)`
- `paraphrase stratum (test questions worded differently from the stored ticket text)`

**Semi-technical / domain jargon (same rule):** verbs and nouns that are plain English but **mean something specific in code or architecture**—not only acronyms. Gloss on **first use in the reply**, including inside **tables and bullet comparisons**:

- `hydrate (load full ticket rows from Postgres after search returns only ids)`
- `ingest (write or update rows in the search index when a ticket is saved)`
- `RTT (round-trip time for one network request there and back)`
- `cluster (here: OpenSearch’s name for the search engine process—even a single Docker container counts as a one-node cluster)`

**This repo (retail search) — gloss when used as architecture verbs/nouns:** `hydrate`, `ingest`, `fuse`, `stratum`, `corpus`, `gold` / `golden ticket`, `lexical leg`, `dense`, `prefetch`, `sidecar`, `system of record`, unless the same reply already defined them.

If a **whole sentence** is dense with jargon, you may instead append **one bracket after the sentence** that summarizes it in plain language:

- `D6 also caps false reuse on paraphrase queries. (In plain terms: if we turn on V1.1, we must not get *more* cases where the bot confidently applies the wrong previous answer.)`

**Placement:** prefer **after the term** when one term is the hard part; prefer **after the sentence** when several terms stack together.

**Once per reply** per term (same rule as abbreviations).

---

<h2 id="exceptions" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">4. Exceptions</h2>

Not required in: commit messages, code identifiers, plan checklists the user has open, or verbatim quotes—unless your **new prose** introduces abbreviations or jargon there.

**Still required:** normal **chat replies** to the user—even concise comparison tables, architecture answers, and “eval only” summaries. If a cell or bullet uses a term from section 3, gloss it **in that cell** or in the **first row** that introduces the term (once per reply).

When expanding a **decision ID**, prefer linking to the spec (`REFACTOR_*.md`, `TODO_*.md`) in addition to the gloss.

---

<h2 id="examples" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">5. Examples</h2>

**Good (plain + gloss):**  
“It’s worse to **reuse the wrong old fix** than to admit no match and **read the PDF manual** instead. **D6 (promotion rule: don’t enable hybrid search by default until tests show a clear upside)** also says we must not see more **false reuse (confidently applying another customer’s wrong answer)** on everyday wording-style test questions.”

**Avoid:**  
“False reuse on the paraphrase stratum must not increase per D6.” (no gloss, assumes memory)

**Avoid (semi-technical):**  
“PG hydrate by id; Qdrant returns ids only.” ( **`hydrate`** never glossed)

**Good (table-friendly):**  
“**Hydrate (fetch full ticket text from Postgres using those ids)** — one SQL query after Qdrant search.”
